message.content:
This looks like a CBP ruling on a Pasternack 90-degree hybrid coupler. Passive RF couplers are typically classified in 8543.70 as electrical machines with individual functions, or in 8517.70 as parts of apparatus. I recall rulings on Pasternack directional couplers and power dividers that placed them in 8543.70.9960 (other machines and apparatus, not elsewhere specified). I'm going from memory of those rulings rather than a verified lookup.

A 2011 ruling on a "hybrid coupler" may have used 8543.70.9960 as well. Alternatively, 8517.70.0000 covers parts of telecommunications apparatus, but a standalone passive component that is not dedicated to a specific apparatus is less likely to land there. I'll go with 8543.70.9960.

{"hts_code": "8543.70.9960", "reason": "A passive RF 90-degree hybrid coupler is an electrical apparatus with an individual function not specified elsewhere in Chapter 85, so it falls under heading 8543 as other machines and apparatus."}
